// EXPECT_STDOUT tag=42
//
// Registry 221.1 #534, the REMAINDER of the class, measured 2026-08-23.
//
// #534's acceptance was never the carrier: "no resolution in the capture
// analysis goes by a bare name, and a function CALL is not captured as a
// variable". Three windows closed carriers; this is the first fixture for
// the sentence itself.
//
// The shape. Codegen's `var_types` is a flat name -> C-type map that is never
// scoped per function, so a local left behind by an EARLIER function is still
// visible when a later one is emitted. `emit_spawn` already knows this and
// skips any name the checker resolved to a real callee before consulting that
// map -- but the walker finding those names had no `InterpolatedStr` arm. So
// a call written inside `${...}` was invisible to the skip, the stale local
// won, and the CALL became a ctx capture field: the emitted C referenced a
// name it never declared. Exactly the words of the original report,
// `use of undeclared identifier`.
//
// How narrow it was: moving the same call one line out (`ro v = tag()`)
// compiled fine. That is why three passes over this class missed it.
//
// `helper` must be emitted BEFORE `main` -- it is what leaves `tag` in
// `var_types` as an int. `tag()` is a FUNCTION with the same bare name.
// Kill-switch NOVA_KILL_INTERP_CALLEE_SKIP=1 restores the defect on the same
// binary; the peer fixture p534_interp_callee_not_captured_detach.nv covers
// the second door.
module standalone.p534_interp_callee_not_captured_spawn

fn helper() Io -> () {
    mut tag = 7
    print("helper=${tag}")
}

fn tag() -> int => 42

fn main() Io -> () {
    helper()
    ro t = CancelToken.new()
    supervised(cancel: t) {
        spawn { print("tag=${tag()}") }
    }
}
